      "writtenOn": "2022-04-26T17:11:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is ~50 lines of code and comments, and it looks like the other Status fields are approximately this complicated. Would it make sense to have a function for each one?",
      "revId": "695efc94a5de8eabe313365d9a5d8611a47d3b91",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "486fb99e_8061df97",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 4310,
      "author": {
        "id": 1527433
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-05-06T00:22:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I moved getting current temperature and temperature status to their own functions. It helps with code organization and allows us to keep the fields in order, as you mentioned in another comment. It is a little slower though. With the previous implementation we just had one loop to go through and read all the temperature sensors to get the temps and compare them to thermal thresholds. Now, we\u0027ll go though and read all the temperature sensors to get the internal temp, then read them all again and compare them to their temperature thresholds to get the temperature status.\n\nThis is all because the PD spec assumes a port has one temperature, but the Chromebooks have multiple temperature sensors, which vary from board to board. In this implementation, we return the highest temperature and the highest exceeded threshold (for example if 3 are \"normal\" but 1 is \"warn\", we return \"warn\").\n\nAnother option would be to chose one temperature sensor as a board config and consider it the only PD-relevant temperature for that board. For example, some boards have a Charger temp sensor which would make sense here.",
      "parentUuid": "2c7b428e_1e8a1e39",
      "revId": "695efc94a5de8eabe313365d9a5d8611a47d3b91",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "40a7c64a_a87ee5f0",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 4310,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287197
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-05-06T20:26:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It\u0027s tricky to say what\u0027s right here.  \n\nReading the spec, there are sections in chapter 7 for both sources and sinks dictating that the OTP bit be set when we overheat and need to send a hard reset message.  And even though our Chromebook may be overheating to the point of throttling the CPU, that doesn\u0027t cause us to be unable to sink/source voltage.  Given the chapter 7 wording, I\u0027d think we\u0027d never report an OTP status because we don\u0027t have conditions for temperature causing us to reset a connection.  \n\nSo I\u0027d guess the charger temperature comes closest to indicating the temperature level the spec cares about?  But the difficulty in using it is that we define our temperature sensor indexes on a per-board basis, and not all boards provide intuitive sensor names (ex. some are just \"TEMP_SENSOR_1\").",
      "parentUuid": "486fb99e_8061df97",
      "revId": "695efc94a5de8eabe313365d9a5d8611a47d3b91",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c13c8b75_34fec91c",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 4310,
      "author": {
        "id": 1527433
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-05-09T16:47:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see, this work on Revision/Status/Alert messages has been to enable power buttons over USB-C. Things like the temperature information is a lower priority. If there is not a clean fit here, do you think it would be better to leave it unsupported? Especially if we are running out of space on some builds.",
      "parentUuid": "40a7c64a_a87ee5f0",
      "revId": "695efc94a5de8eabe313365d9a5d8611a47d3b91",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e6e55284_d3e65eb4",
        "filename": "common/usbc/usb_pe_drp_sm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 4310,
      "author": {
        "id": 1305927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-05-09T20:35:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Re: redundant work, I\u0027m not to worried about it, but I think you could sidestep the issue by first figuring out our \"worst\" temperature in a separate function and then setting both fields on that basis. We have different thresholds for different sensors, but if, e.g. our CPU is fine at 70C, and our charger chip is dying at 50C, it\u0027s probably OK if we send back 50C in the internal temp field.\n\nI think just picking the charger temp would also be a pretty good option here. We can have multiple chargers, though, so it\u0027s not completely straightforward.\n\nRe: priority, I\u0027d say any of the designs discussed so far are close enough for now.",
      "parentUuid": "c13c8b75_34fec91c",
      "revId": "695efc94a5de8eabe313365d9a5d8611a47d3b91",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
